#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087571 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087755 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23c4516d-ba9c-5b55-b971-ceeb575d50c3" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Factor Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "01993d51-8a9f-5156-b5cc-2784e93da5aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-5753" :question/problem "Determine if $x+3$ is a factor of $p(x)=x^3+3x^2-3x-9$." :question/worked-solution "According to the factor theorem, $(x-r)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x = r$ is also a root of $p(x)$.\n\nHere, we are considering $(x+3)$. The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 3 &= 0 \\\\\nx &= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, then, $(x+3)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x = -3$ is a root of $p(x)$.\n\nSo, we just need to check if $x=-3$ is a root of $p(x)$. In other words, we need to check if $p(-3)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(-3) &= (-3)^{3} + 3(-3)^{2} - 3(-3) - 9 \\\\\n&=-27 + 27 + 9 - 9 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, $p(-3)=0$, so $x = -3$ is a root of the polynomial. Consequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x+3)$ is indeed a factor of $p(x)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087572 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087756 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd5d3a7d-ef95-5d7d-a92a-ce76d6b400bb" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Factor Theorem to Factors With Leading Coefficients"}] :question/id #uuid "da4e2c42-894b-5f2a-917b-a5df22f41019" :question/math-academy-id "e-5754" :question/problem "Determine if $(4-3x)$ is a factor of $p(x)=-6x^2+11x-4$." :question/worked-solution "According to the factor theorem, $(ax-b)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{b}{a}\n$$\n\nis also a root of $p(x)$.\n\nHere, we are considering $(4-3x)$. The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 - 3x &= 0 \\\\\n4 &= 3x \\\\\nx &= \\frac{4}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, then, $(4-3x)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nis also a root of $p(x)$.\n\nSo, we just need to check if\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nis a root of $p(x)$. In other words, we need to check if\n\n$$\np\\left(\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right) =0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(\\frac{4}{3}) &= -6(\\frac{4}{3})^{2} + 11(\\frac{4}{3}) - 4 \\\\\n&=-6 \\cdot \\frac{16}{9} + \\frac{44}{3} - 4 \\\\\n&= \\frac{-32 + 44 - 12}{3} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed,\n\n$$\np \\left(\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)=0\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nis a root of the polynomial. Consequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(4-3x)$ is indeed a factor of $p(x)$."}]}
